 with EXPLAIN ANALYZE\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:chil"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"dren:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n6f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Once you have identified your problematic queries, you can use \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://thoughtbot.com/blog/reading-an-explain-analyze-query-plan\",\"children\":\"EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)\"}],\" on them to dive deeper into why they are slow:\"]}]\n71:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Consider this hypothetical output:\"}]\n73:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"This would tell you a lot. For example: the execution plan begins with a \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.pgmustard.com/docs/explain/sequential-scan\",\"children\":\"sequential scan\"}],\" (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Seq Scan\"}],\") of the sales table, followed by sorting (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"Sort\"}],\") the results on \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"customer_id\"}],\", and then performing a grouping operation (\",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"GroupAggregate\"}],\"). This indicates that the query scans the entire table to find the relevant rows, which is not efficient – especially if your table is getting large.\"]}]\n74:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"improving-query-performance-in-neon-postgres\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Improving query performance in Neon Postgres\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#improving-query-performance-in-neon-postgres\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Improving query performance in Neon Postgres\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n75:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Once you have an idea of where things may be wrong, you can try these tactics:\"}]\n76:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"add-new-indexes-if-you-need-them\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Add new indexes (if you need them)\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#add-new-indexes-if-you-need-them\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Add new indexes (if you need them)\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n77:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If we go back to the previous example, given the insights from EXPLAIN ANALYZE, you might decide to add an index on \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"customer_id\"}],\", especially if that was a query you run often:\"]}]\n79:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"After implementing this index, rerunning \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"EXPLAIN ANALYZE\"}],\" on the query should show that Postgres now performs an index scan instead of a sequential scan, which should reduce the execution time.\"]}]\n7a:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"reduce-bloat\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Reduce bloat\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#reduce-bloat\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Reduce bloat\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n7b:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"After ruling out indexes, the next thing to consider would be table bloat. Managing bloat can be (unfortunately) \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://rbranson.medium.com/10-things-i-hate-about-postgresql-20dbab8c2791\",\"children\":\"quite a pain in Postgres\"}],\", so it really pays off to keep an eye on it. A bloated table not only will be less performant, but it will also need much more storage than a healthy table – \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/6-tips-to-optimize-storage-costs-for-your-postgres-databases\",\"children\":\"therefore inflating your bill.\"}]]}]\n7c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Let’s go back to our previous EXPLAIN example output. Imagine we already had an index in customer_id; its absence in the plan might indicate that the index is inefficient, possibly due to bloat. A bloated index can lead Postgres to default back to a full table scan.\"}]\n7d:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"To fix this, you could consider a strategy with two parts:\"}]\n7e:[\"$\",\"ul\",null,{\"children\":[\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"First, you would fix your inefficient index by running the \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresqltutorial.com/postgresql-indexes/postgresql-reindex/\",\"children\":\"REINDEX\"}],\" command. This command will rebuild your index from scratch. Beware: this is an intensive operation, so plan to do this during a maintenance period.\"]}],\"\\n\",[\"$\",\"li\",null,{\"children\":[\"Second, you would prevent this from happening again by addressing table bloat regularly. Consider \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.percona.com/blog/tuning-autovacuum-in-postgresql-and-autovacuum-internals/\",\"children\":\"fine-tuning your autovacuum settings\"}],\", for example adjust parameters such as \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"autovacuum_vacuum_scale_factor\"}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"autovacuum_vacuum_threshold\"}],\". Yon can also schedule regular bloat check-ups as part of your Postgres maintenance routine. For example, the \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/16/pgstattuple.html\",\"children\":\"pgstattuple\"}],\" extension provides functions to show table and index bloat levels.\"]}],\"\\n\"]}]\n7f:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"cache-your-data\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Cache your data\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#cache-your-data\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Cache your data\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n80:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"If you optimize indexes and reduce bloat but still observe suboptimal performance, checking cache usage could be a good next step.\"}]\n81:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Ensuring that data you frequently access is predominantly served from cache can mitigate performance issues not solved by indexing alone. This is especially important in Neon, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/architecture-decisions-in-neon\",\"children\":\"given its unique architecture that extends Postgres shared buffers with a local file cache.\"}],\" The process of analyzing cache usage therefore looks a bit different in Neon vs in traditional Postgres.\"]}]\n82:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"To evaluate caching effectiveness in Neon, you need to look at local file cache hits instead of shared buffer hits. Neon allows you to do this by providing a \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"neon_stat_file_cache\"}],\" view. To access this view, you need to install the neon extension first:\"]}]\n84:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"After your workload has been running and "])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"generating data, you can analyze your cache hit ratio by running this command:\"}]\n86:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Your output might look like this:\"}]\n88:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"If the hit ratio falls below the ideal threshold (99% in Neon), \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/docs/manage/endpoints#how-to-size-your-compute\",\"children\":\"reconsider your compute configuration – e.g. increase your autoscaling limits.\"}],\" Often, the reason behind this issue is inefficient memory allocation, which is a simple thing to fix.\"]}]\n89:[\"$\",\"h2\",null,{\"id\":\"optimizing-ingestion-rates-in-neon-postgres\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-tighter\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Optimizing ingestion rates in Neon Postgres\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#optimizing-ingestion-rates-in-neon-postgres\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Optimizing ingestion rates in Neon Postgres\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3.5\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n8a:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Now that we’ve talked about how to diagnose and fix slow queries, it’s time to cover writes. If you care about ingesting as much data as possible into your Postgres database, here are some simple strategies that can get you a long way.\"}]\n8b:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"make-sure-your-client-has-enough-compute\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Make sure your client has enough compute\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#make-sure-your-client-has-enough-compute\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Make sure your client has enough compute\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n8c:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"This is a basic, but it happens. At the start of this post, we mentioned how it is important to make sure your Neon instances have enough resources to enable good performance; but to optimize ingests, the resources available on the client side are also important. Ensure that you have enough CPU, memory, and bandwidth in your client to handle the data load efficiently. Also, try hosting your Neon project as close as possible (geographically) to your client.\"}]\n8d:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"use-read-replicas\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Use read replicas\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#use-read-replicas\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Use read replicas\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n8e:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"This tip could also live in the query performance section since it helps both with writes and reads. If your application deals with heavy writes, heavy read queries, or both, Neon allows you to liberate l"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"oad in your main compute by offloading read queries to a \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/docs/introduction/read-replicas\",\"children\":\"read-only compute endpoint\"}],\", which is equivalent to the concept of read replica in other databases.\"]}]\n8f:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The main difference between Neon’s implementation and “regular” read replicas resides in the serverless nature of Neon. In Neon, \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/blog/white-widgets-secret-to-scalable-postgres-neon\",\"children\":\"read replicas are ephemeral\"}],\": they don’t need their own copy of storage, they’re ready instantly when you need them, and they scale down to zero when traffic slows down. They’re much more affordable than traditional read replicas – \",[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"href\":\"https://neon.tech/docs/guides/read-replica-guide\",\"children\":\"take advantage of them.\"}]]}]\n90:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"insert-data-in-batches\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit scroll-mt-20 leading-tight font-medium lg:scroll-mt-5 tracking-extra-tight\",\"children\":[[\"$\",\"span\",null,{\"children\":[\"Insert data in batches\"]}],[\"$\",\"a\",null,{\"className\":\"rounded-full px-2 no-underline opacity-0 transition-opacity duration-200 group-hover:opacity-100 hover:border-none hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 focus-visible:outline-offset-0 sm:hidden\",\"href\":\"#insert-data-in-batches\",\"aria-label\":\"Link to Insert data in batches\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"svg\",null,{\"xmlns\":\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\",\"width\":14,\"height\":14,\"fill\":\"none\",\"viewBox\":\"0 0 14 14\",\"className\":\"inline text-green-44 dark:text-green-52 w-3\",\"children\":\"$57:4:props:children:1:props:children:props:children\"}]}]]}]\n91:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"One of the most simple yet effective strategies for improving ingestion rates is batch insert operations. Instead of inserting rows one by one, group them into larger transactions.\"}]\n92:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"For example, instead of executing individual \",[\"$\",\"code\",null,{\"children\":\"INSERT\"}],\" statements for each row:\"]}]\n94:[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":\"Combine them into a single statement:\"}]\n96:[\"$\",\"h3\",null,{\"id\":\"clean-up-old-indexes\",\"className\":\"anchor-heading group relative w-fit sc